%% DM26-0670

%% Where jira-observer's first pull starts, read back through the state agent, as built (2026-11-04, docs/architecture/soft-state.md, "The Jira cursor",
%% docs/architecture/jira.md). Seen on u7s the same day: "the graph's newest issue was updated at ... ms; the first pull starts there". It is a hint, kept apart from what is signed.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Proc as jira-processor
    participant Graph as Neo4j
    participant Issues as Jira issue observer
    participant State as StateActor<br/>(in jira-observer)
    participant Cassini
    participant Reader as soft-state-observer
    participant Jira as Jira, through the protocol handler

    Proc->>Graph: each JiraIssue with updated_ms, read from the system field updated
    Note over Issues: Started, or told the broker ended its session<br/>(Resync), which forgets where it was
    Issues->>State: get Max { JiraIssue, updated_ms }

    loop until an answer, each attempt with a fresh correlation id
        State-)Cassini: soft-state-requests, stamped with its identity
        Cassini-)Reader: the request, with the identity the broker stamped
        Reader->>Reader: a verified asker, and plain identifiers. Then Cedar, Action read-state on the label,<br/>then the floor, its signed runtime section schema
        alt admitted
            Reader->>Graph: MATCH (n:`JiraIssue`) WHERE n.`updated_ms` IS NOT NULL RETURN max(n.`updated_ms`)
            Graph-->>Reader: the largest, or none
            Reader-)Cassini: Value, on soft-state-replies.jira-observer
        else not admitted
            Reader-)Cassini: Refused, naming only what the asker sent
        else no schema yet, or the graph or policy could not answer
            Reader-)Cassini: Unavailable
        end
        Cassini-)State: the reply. Counted only if the broker says the state agent published it
        Note over State: No reply in 8 s is a new attempt, 3 in all.<br/>Unavailable is asked again every 10 s, for up to 120 s
    end
    State-->>Issues: the value, or why there is none

    alt Value(Some(t))
        Issues->>Jira: updated >= -Nm, N the minutes back to t plus the 300 s overlap, rounded up
    else Value(None): the graph has no issue
        Issues->>Jira: a full pull, or from QUERY_DATE, which applies only here
    else Refused, Unavailable or no reply
        Note over Issues: Asked again on the observer's backoff. Never taken for an empty graph,<br/>since a full pull is what the cursor is there to avoid
    end
    Note over Issues,Jira: Every pull orders by updated ASC, so a pull cut short leaves the graph's newest meaning everything older was written.<br/>Relative, so the Jira user's time zone does not enter, and the overlap covers the skew between the clocks
    Jira-->>Issues: pages of issues, published for jira-processor
